b2a2c05-4fc1-5b07-8524-1e5c2d56ee47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091674 #edb/ref 1080 "2.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091674 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ee0ed44-8439-52fb-8cbd-aff250e3d290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091675 #edb/ref 1080 "-2.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091675 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091675 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "384641ba-762f-5d21-a28e-551e7dbde5f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091676 #edb/ref 1080 "25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091676 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091676 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5fb796b2-7a9f-57ec-828d-6e37178f6662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091677 #edb/ref 1080 "0.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091677 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091677 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6ec255b0-f630-5bd5-9b6f-66bf1c5792cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091955 #edb/ref 1069 "e-151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091955 #edb/ref 1071 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1.0$ | $-0.7$ | $-0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1.5$ | $2.1$ |\n\nApproximate  at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7f1c431-54b4-52b5-9ad6-cb4dd81e0f30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091955 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=3.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+1)-f(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-0.3)-(-0.7)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{1} \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091956 #edb/ref 1069 "e-152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091956 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown below. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$\n\n![](images/30/3084d6ae67f37517f3d8ad0a9a62be05fe1906fcc2a24f69bd29a1e00dfd0373.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8717eda6-57e6-59ec-ab1c-f40887fa52fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091956 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=4.$  Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+2)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091957 #edb/ref 1069 "e-153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091957 #edb/ref 1071 "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "580533af-c13d-5e1c-8282-f0172709c7e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091957 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t+h) - s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=0.1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.1) & ≈\\frac{s(0.1+0.1)-s(0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.2)-s(0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-3}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.1} \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=0.1$ the velocity is approximately $40\\, \\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091958 #edb/ref 1069 "e-154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091958 #edb/ref 1071 "An oil tank has leaked $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours. The graph of $q(t)$ is shown below. Approximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c5286dae7e739a588173bad9c721ae34d07f86e455e44ec9f229628cf54211.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73818e0b-6f95-59a6-b1a7-672bbea8de86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091958 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\nq'(t) \\approx \\frac{q(t+h) - q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ hour, the tank is leaking oil at a rate of approximately $1\\,\\textrm{liter}/ \\textrm{hour}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092386 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation" #edb/ref 1759218604541