 | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $n(t)$ | $851$ | $856$ | $861$ | $865$ | $870$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of growth in population in the second year $(t = 2)$ using a forward difference approximation.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96520ca3-f98e-5eb7-82a1-1aa5774145a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092716 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092717 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092717 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092717 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092717 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $s$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(s)$ | $15$ | $13.5$ | $12.1$ | $9.4$ | $8.2$ | $6.6$ | $4.8$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s=8$ miles using a forward difference approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092717 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95a18afa-f8fa-5bdd-82d9-85c9a941305b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092717 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092717 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(s)≈\\frac{g(s+h)-f(s)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $s=8.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(8) & ≈\\frac{g(8+2)-g(8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(10)-g(8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.6-8.2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1.6}{2} \\\\  & =-0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $s=8$ the rate at which the fuel is changing is approximately $-0.8\\,\\text{liters/mile}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092719 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092719 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092719 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f4/f43c53ce3804de0ec59ca135a97f11e50f1022c129d87f4ea0df7bce4665d130.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g^{′}(x)$ at the point $x = 2$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h = 1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0abb86c-6c26-5523-a3a8-a9c4c1e1ae48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092912 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092912 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092912 #edb/ref 1071 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $10.5$ | $6$ | $1.4$ | $-1$ | $-2.5$ |\n\nApproximate $f^{′}(x)$ at the point $x =-4$ using a forward difference approximation.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092912 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "923940f3-d99f-59e8-8aa1-3dcaca05cfa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092912 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093330 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093330 #edb/ref 1061 676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093330 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0976a2b-1337-5627-8c4d-ef63079d733d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093330 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093331 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093331 #edb/ref 1061 677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093331 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f68f148f-5e8f-52a7-ae44-4bf6aabecc15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093331 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093332 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093332 #edb/ref 1061 680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa713ca3-ee27-5c87-897f-349e9d84433e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093333 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093333 #edb/ref 1061 682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093333 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e8c5132-4661-56c0-9cc7-9cd625cd092f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093333 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093334 #edb/ref 1061 684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093334 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "712fc50f-c422-5419-9b82-fa1b3e7a4a00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093334 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093443 #edb/ref 1064 "For a function $f(x),$ its derivative at a point $x$ is defined by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}.\n$$\n\nBut often, computing this limit exactly is impossible or impractical. One example might be when we don't have an explicit expression for $f(x).$ In such cases, instead of finding an exact value for $f'(x)$, we can *approximate* the derivative using the formula\n\n \n\n$$\nf'(x)\\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}\n$$\n\nfor small values of the \"step size\" $h,$ with $h>0.$\n\nThis expression is called the **forward difference approximation** because if $h >0$ then $x+h$ is a step forward (i.e., to the right) from $x.$\n\n![](images/18/1860d33c3b34fa5c869f5a3c7a04ef4d02271ba30d60f973b892800ef7c9524e.png)\n\nGenerally, this and other similar methods are known as **finite-difference** approximations. The smaller the value of $h,$ the better the approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093443 #edb/ref 1065 363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093443 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c3fb3f8f-2a89-5f4a-8f24-9cf556a18c41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093443 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315362 #edb/ref 1080 "-40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315362 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 